course: world
lesson: 194
title: Olympiad: everyday reasoning ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Everyday puzzles hide in socks, fences, queues and stairs. The quick answer is usually the trap; the right one comes from picturing the real thing, step by step.
explain: Counting things and counting the gaps between them are different: a 10-metre fence with a post every 2 metres has 5 gaps but 6 posts, and cutting a log into 4 pieces takes only 3 cuts.
explain: Some puzzles turn on the worst case. Grabbing socks in the dark from a drawer of red, blue and black ones, 3 socks could all be different colours, but the 4th must match one of them.
explain: Some puzzles are best solved backwards: if lily pads double every day and cover the pond on day 30, then the day before, day 29, they covered half of it.
explain: Real talk adds a last trap. "The queue was a mile long" and "I'm starving" are hyperbole, not numbers; "it's just a scratch" may hide a real injury, and then the wise move is to check and tell an adult.
fact: In the dark, from a drawer of red, blue and black socks, taking 4 socks is enough to be sure of a matching pair; 3 could all be different.
fact: A 10-metre fence with a post every 2 metres, counting both ends, needs 6 posts.
fact: Cutting a log into 4 pieces takes 3 cuts, so at 2 minutes a cut it takes 6 minutes.
fact: Going from floor 1 to floor 3 is 2 flights of stairs; at 15 seconds a flight, floor 1 to floor 6 is 5 flights, 75 seconds.
fact: A snail in a 10-metre well that climbs 3 metres each day and slips back 2 metres each night gets out on day 8, because on the last day it doesn't slip back.
fact: Lily pads that double every day and cover a pond on day 30 covered half of it on day 29.
fact: If you are 5th from the front and 5th from the back of a queue, the queue has 9 people, because you are counted in both.
fact: When 4 friends each shake hands once with each other, there are 6 handshakes.
fact: If Ella has 3 brothers and each brother has 1 sister, that sister is Ella, so there are 4 children.
fact: Among 9 coins with one heavier fake, a balance finds the fake in 2 weighings: weigh 3 against 3, then 1 against 1 from the heavier three, or from the three left out if it balances.
fact: Trains every 20 minutes from 7:10 leave at 7:30, 7:50, 8:10 and 8:30, so someone arriving at 8:15 has missed one by 5 minutes and waits 15.
fact: "It's just a scratch" about a cut that won't stop bleeding is downplaying a real injury; it needs pressure and an adult.
fact: Words people use: "bare" (London) means lots of; "proper job" (West Country) means well done; "arvo" (Australia) means afternoon; "lekker" (South Africa) means great; "defo" means definitely.
exercise: In the dark Sam grabs socks from a drawer of red, blue and black ones. How many must he take to be sure of a matching pair? | 4 | 3 | 2 | 6
exercise: A 10-metre fence has a post every 2 metres, one at each end too. How many posts? | 6 | 10 | 12 | 20
exercise: Each cut through a log takes 2 minutes. How long to cut it into 4 pieces? | 6 minutes | 8 minutes | 4 minutes | 10 minutes
exercise: Walking from floor 1 to floor 3 takes 30 seconds. How long from floor 1 to floor 6 at the same speed? | 75 seconds | 90 seconds | 60 seconds | 45 seconds
exercise: A snail in a 10-metre well climbs 3 metres each day and slips back 2 metres each night. On which day does it get out? | Day 8 | Day 10 | Day 7 | Day 5
exercise: Lily pads double every day and cover the pond on day 30. On which day did they cover half of it? | Day 29 | Day 15 | Day 28 | Day 20
exercise: Raj is 5th from the front and 5th from the back of a queue. How many people are in it? | 9 | 10 | 11 | 12
exercise: 4 friends each shake hands once with each other. How many handshakes? | 6 | 12 | 16 | 10
exercise: Ella has 3 brothers. Each brother has 1 sister. How many children are there? | 4 | 6 | 7 | 5
exercise: 3 apples cost 60p. How much do 5 apples cost? | £1 | 80p | £1.20 | 90p
exercise: 9 coins look the same, but one fake is heavier. How few weighings on a balance always find it? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 8
exercise: "The queue was a mile long!" What does the speaker most likely mean? | It was a very long queue | It was one mile exactly | It was a short queue | It was 1,000 metres long
exercise: Kim is older than Raj but younger than Tess. Who is in the middle? | Kim | Raj | Tess | You can't tell
exercise: Trains leave every 20 minutes from 7:10. Ana gets to the station at 8:15. When is the next train? | 8:30 | 8:20 | 8:35 | 8:10
exercise: Your friend's cut won't stop bleeding, and he says, "It's just a scratch." What is wise? | Press on it and get an adult | Believe him and play on | Plaster it, say nothing | Wait and see
test: Noah, in the dark: "Red, blue and black socks in here. I'll grab 3, that's defo a pair." What is true? | 3 could be all different; 4 makes sure | 3 is always enough for a pair of socks | He needs 6 to be sure of a pair | He needs 2, because socks come in pairs
test: Dad, West Country: "Fence is 10 metre, a post every 2, so 5 posts. Proper job." What has he missed? | The post at the start: it is 6 | Nothing; 10 ÷ 2 is 5 posts | The posts in the middle: it is 10 | The gaps: it is 4
test: Finn: "I'm literally starving, haven't eaten for like a hundred years." He had lunch at 1 and it's 5. What does he mean? | He's very hungry after four hours | He hasn't eaten for a hundred years | He's not hungry at all | He has been ill for a long time
test: Ollie: "Snail goes up 3, slides 2, so 1 metre a day. 10-metre well, 10 days, no cap." What is the real answer? | Day 8; on the last day it climbs out before it can slip | Day 10; one metre a day for ten metres | Day 5; it climbs 3 metres every day | It never gets out; it always slides back
test: Priya: "Pond's covered on day 30, so it's half covered on day 15, easy peasy." Put her right. | Day 29; they double, so half is one day before | Day 15 is right, since 15 is half of 30 | Day 28, because half of half is a quarter | Day 1, since they started that day
test: Mia at the footy in the arvo: "I'm 5th from the front and 5th from the back, so there's 10 of us, heaps." How many are there? | 9; she was counted twice | 10, as she said | 11, counting her once extra | 5, because both counts match
test: Thabo from Cape Town: "Lekker party! Four of us shook hands with everyone, so 16 handshakes." How many were there? | 6; a handshake needs two hands and nobody shakes their own | 16, four times four | 12; each shakes three hands | 8, two each
test: Grace: "My mate says she's got 3 brothers and every brother has a sister, so they're 7 kids." How many really? | 4; every brother has the same sister, her | 7, as Grace worked out | 6, three brothers and three sisters | 5, because her mum counts as one
test: Jayden, after falling off his bike: "It's nothing, bare blood but whatever, keep riding." The cut won't stop. What should his friend do? | Press on it and get an adult now | Keep riding, as he says it's nothing | Laugh it off; boys always say that | Wait an hour and see how it looks
test: Kai: "Fake coin's heavier. 9 coins, so I'll weigh one by one. 8 goes, innit." What is the smart way? | 3 against 3, then 1 against 1: 2 weighings | One against one each time: 8 weighings | Weigh all 9 at once: 1 weighing | 4 against 4, then 2 against 2: 3 weighings
test: Ana, at 8:15: "Missed it by a mile, typical!" Trains go every 20 minutes from 7:10. What really happened? | She missed the 8:10 by 5 minutes; next is 8:30 | She missed the train by one whole mile | She missed the 7:50; next is 8:10 | She's early; the next is at 8:20
exam: A drawer contains socks of three colours. Determine the least number which must be drawn unseen to ensure a matching pair. | Four | Three | Two | Six
exam: A fence of ten metres in length bears a post every two metres, including one at each extremity. State the number of posts. | Six | Five | Ten | Four
exam: Four persons each shake hands once with every other. Ascertain the total number of handshakes. | Six | Twelve | Sixteen | Four
exam: A covering of lily pads doubles daily and completes the pond on the thirtieth day. Identify the day on which the pond was half covered. | The twenty-ninth | The fifteenth | The twenty-eighth | The first
